narrative:
At the village gate, Bailiff Nera is pinning red debt-ribbons to three doors. The ribbons are new; the faces behind the doors are not. A miller named Joren argues that a failed crop should not count as a broken promise. Nera keeps her voice soft, which makes the words crueller.

@Nera: The Crown's grain office does not accept weather as payment.

@Joren: Then it should try eating paperwork.

The crowd sees you preparing to leave. For one breath, they expect you to be the sort of person who can make officials listen. It is unfair. It is also the first thing the road asks of you.

Below the grove, a child from the reed settlements is trying to free a paper boat from a culvert. The boat bears a charcoal sun with a dark circle scratched through it. A nursery charm, perhaps. Or a copied sign from somewhere no child should have seen.

@Child: It is sailing to the king. It has to tell him the river tastes wrong.

You kneel in the mud. The water has the same metallic edge as the blight-wind. Not enough to poison a cup. Enough to make an old memory wake up and refuse to lie down.

Sael meets you again at the road, though you never heard her follow. She has tied red thread around the grip of your bow—the lowland color, not yours.

@Sael: Their grief will not speak our language. Learn it anyway.

You want to ask whether she is afraid. Instead, you ask whether she will tell the council you left.

@Sael: I will tell them the truth. That is why they will be angry.

The lowlands open below you, crowded with smoke, fields, and brief human lives. You have time enough to mourn every one of them. That is not the same as having the right to arrive late.

The innkeeper finds you at the stable door with your cloak already fastened. Her name is Sella; you know because the child at dinner called it when a spoon fell. She does not ask what you are. That restraint is a kind of bravery.

@Sella: You noticed the girl. Her brother is at the farms. He has not come home in four days.

The pulse in her throat is fast, but not from fear of you. That unsettles you more than fear would. She holds out a small brass button stamped with a sheaf of wheat.

@Sella: If you find Tomas, give him this. Tell him I have kept his soup from burning.

The button is warm from her palm. You remember the button you kept for reasons you never explain to anyone.

You take the apprentice to a public fountain. Crowds are safer than alleys, even when crowds are cruel. The apprentice keeps glancing at the faint ember-light beneath your collar.

@Apprentice: Are you one of them?

The question has been asked with knives, prayers, curiosity, and once by a child holding a dead sparrow. It is never the same question twice.

@You: I am the person who stopped you. Start there.

The apprentice nods as if given a lesson, not an evasion. Then a temple warden turns the corner. His eyes go straight to the pendant.
